problems.solution:
The kernel body is one line — dereference the device pointer and store:

```cuda
#include <cstdio>
#include <cuda_runtime.h>

// Runs on the GPU. One thread executes it.
__global__ void answer(int *out) {
    *out = 42;
}

int main() {
    cudaDeviceProp prop;
    cudaGetDeviceProperties(&prop, 0);
    printf("device: %s\n", prop.name);
    printf("SMs: %d\n", prop.multiProcessorCount);

    int *dResult;
    cudaMalloc(&dResult, sizeof(int));       // allocate on the device
    answer<<<1, 1>>>(dResult);               // launch 1 block of 1 thread
    int host = 0;
    cudaMemcpy(&host, dResult, sizeof(int), cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost);
    cudaFree(dResult);

    printf("kernel result: %d\n", host);
    return 0;
}
```

**Why the copy is needed.** `dResult` is an address in GPU memory. Dereferencing it on the host would segfault, so `cudaMemcpy` moves the four bytes back across the PCIe bus into `host`.

**Why `<<<1, 1>>>`.** The first number is blocks, the second is threads per block. Here one thread is enough — the next problem launches 65,536 of them.

The SM count is the number of Streaming Multiprocessors, the independent scheduling units on the card. Every block you launch is assigned to one of them.

